Name":{"value":"Two men on the shore of the Dead Sea, NINO F Scholten Doode Zee 3","source":"mediawiki-metadata"},"CommonsMetadataExtension":{"value":1.2,"source":"extension","hidden":""},"Categories":{"value":"PD US expired|Author died more than 80 years ago public domain images|CC-PD-Mark|Photographs by Frank Scholten (flat list)|Photographs of adults by Frank Scholten|Photographs of the Dead Sea by Frank Scholten","source":"commons-categories","hidden":""},"Assessments":{"value":"","source":"commons-categories","hidden":""},"ImageDescription":{"value":"<table class=\"wikitable unsortable\">\n\n\n\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>Extent\n</th>\n<td>electronic resource\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<th>Title\n</th>\n<td>Two men on the shore of the Dead Sea, NINO F Scholten Doode Zee 3\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<th>Creator/other\n</th>\n<td>Scholten, Frank (1881-1942)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<th>Shelfmark\n</th>\n<td>NINO F Scholten Doode Zee 3\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<th>Subject (topical)\n</th>\n<td>Cellulose nitrate filmLandscapes (representations)<br>Shores (landforms)<br>Seas<br>Folk costume<br>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<th>Subject (geographic)\n</th>\n<td>Dead Sea (salt lake)Yam HaMela\u1e96<br>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<th>Note\n</th>\n<td>During this period, Frank Scholten visited the Dead Sea 2 times. The duration of his visits was one day. Original file was named \"Doode Zee\", the third word is illegible. \"Scholten\" written on the negative.Title devised from content by Library Staff.<br>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<th>Reference\n</th>\n<td>T.L. Kwiecie\u0144, \u2018Frank Scholten\u2019, in: Fotolexicon, vol. 25, nr. 40, <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://depthoffield.universiteitleiden.nl/2540f05en/S\">https://depthoffield.universiteitleiden.nl/2540f05en/S</a>. Zananiri, \u2018Frank Scholten. Landschap in het Brits Mandaat Palestina\u2019, in: Fotografisch Geheugen 96 (2018) 3, pages 26-30<br>S. Zananiri, \u2018Documenting the Social: Frank Scholten Taxonomising Identity in British Mandate Palestine', in: K.S. Sanchez Summerer and S. Zananiri (eds.), Imaging and Imagining Palestine. Leiden 2021, pages 266-306<br>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<th>Language\n</th>\n<td>No linguistic content\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<th>Country\n</th>\n<td>Netherlands\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<th>Published/created\n</th>\n<td>[Between 02 January 1922 and 10 March 1923]\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<th>Persistent URL\n</th>\n<td><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"http://hdl.handle.net/1887.1/item:3426976\">http://hdl.handle.net/1887.1/item:3426976</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<th>Published (digital)\n</th>\n<td>Leiden University Libraries,\n</td></tr></tbody></table>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"DateTimeOriginal":{"value":"Between 02 January 1922 and 10 March 1923","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Credit":{"value":"<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"http://hdl.handle.net/1887.1/item:3426976\">http://hdl.handle.net/1887.1/item:3426976</a>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Artist":{"value":"Frank Scholten","source":"commons-desc-page"},"LicenseShortName":{"value":"Public domain","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"UsageTerms":{"value":"Public domain","source":"commons-desc-page"},"AttributionRequired":{"value":"false","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"Copyrighted":{"value":"False","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"Restrictions":{"value":"","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"License":{"value":"pd","source":"commons-templates","hidden":""}}}]},"31947749":{"pageid":31947749,"ns":6,"title":"File:VIDEO - Authorised swimming zone on the Dead Sea shore (11737770084).jpg","index":6,"imagerepository":"local","imageinfo":[{"size":147297,"width":1920,"height":1080,"thumburl":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ee/VIDEO_-_Authorised_swimming_zone_on_the_Dead_Sea_shore_%2811737770084%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","thumbwidth":1600,"thumbheight":900,"thumbattribs":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ee/VIDEO_-_Authorised_swimming_zone_on_the_Dead_Sea_shore_%2811737770084%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","decoding":"async","loading":"lazy","width":1600,"height":900},"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ee/VIDEO_-_Authorised_swimming_zone_on_the_Dead_Sea_shore_%2811737770084%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","descriptionurl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:VIDEO_-_Authorised_swimming_zone_on_the_Dead_Sea_shore_(11737770084).jpg","descriptionshorturl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=31947749","extmetadata":{"DateTime":{"value":"2014-04-02 06:30:57","source":"mediawiki-metadata","hidden":""},"ObjectName":{"value":"VIDEO - Authorised swimming zone on the Dead Sea shore (11737770084)","source":"mediawiki-metadata"},"CommonsMetadataExtension":{"value":1.2,"source":"extension","hidden":""},"Categories":{"value":"Flickr images reviewed by FlickreviewR|Files with coordinates missing SDC location of creation|Israel photographs taken on 2014-01-03|Bathing people at the Dead Sea|Files from Jorge L\u00e1scar Flickr stream","source":"commons-categories","hidden":""},"Assessments":{"value":"","source":"commons-categories","hidden":""},"GPSLatitude":{"value":"31.203000","source":"commons-desc-page"},"GPSLongitude":{"value":"35.364833","source":"commons-desc-page"},"GPSMapDatum":{"value":"WGS-84","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"ImageDescription":{"value":"The Dead Sea, also called the Salt Sea, is a salt lake bordering Jordan to the east, and Israel and Palestine to the west. Its surface and shores are 427 metres (1,401 ft) below sea level, Earth's lowest elevation on land. The Dead Sea is 306 m (1,004 ft) deep, the deepest hypersaline lake in the world. With 34.2% salinity (in 2011), it is also one of the world's saltiest bodies of water, though Lake Vanda Antarctica (35%), Lake Assal (Djibouti) (34.8%), Lagoon Garabogazk\u00f6l in the Caspian Sea (up to 35%) and some hypersaline ponds and lakes of the McMurdo Dry Valleys in Antarctica (such as Don Juan Pond (44%)) have reported higher salinities. It is 9.6 times as salty as the ocean. This salinity makes for a harsh environment in which animals cannot flourish, hence its name. The Dead Sea is 50 kilometres (31 mi) long and 15 kilometres (9 mi) wide at its widest point. It lies in the Jordan Rift Valley, and its main tributary is the Jordan River [Wikipedia.org]","source":"commons-desc-page"},"DateTimeOriginal":{"value":"2014-01-03 12:34","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Credit":{"value":"<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.flickr.com/photos/jlascar/11737770084/\">VIDEO - Authorised swimming zone on the Dead Sea shore</a>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Artist":{"value":"<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.flickr.com/people/8721758@N06\">Jorge L\u00e1scar</a> from Australia","source":"commons-desc-page"},"LicenseShortName":{"value":"CC BY 2.0","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"UsageTerms":{"value":"Creative Commons Attribution 2.0","source":"commons-desc-page"},"AttributionRequired":{"value":"true","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"LicenseUrl":{"value":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Copyrighted":{"value":"True","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"Restrictions":{"value":"","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"License":{"value":"cc-by-2.0","source":"commons-templates","hidden":""}}}]}}}}